  "summary": "Architecture that seeks to connect building occupants more closely with nature through design elements and natural systems.",
  "description": "Characterized by abundant natural light, indoor plants, water features, natural materials, and views of nature, often incorporating fractal patterns and biomimetic forms. It developed from environmental psychology and biophilia research, recognizing humans' innate need to connect with nature for wellbeing and productivity.",
  "culturalContext": "Biophilic design — E.O. Wilson's \"biophilia\" (1984) turned architectural doctrine — integrates nature into buildings for measurable wellbeing: Singapore's Parkroyal and Oasia hotels with planted sky terraces, Milan's Bosco Verticale, Amazon Spheres, plus the 14-pattern framework (Browning/Ryan/Clancy, 2014) codifying views of nature, natural materials, light rhythms and airflow. Green-building economics (productivity, recovery speed) made it policy; the aesthetic reads as architecture surrendering its edges to living systems.",
